  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...\n\nThe strong weekend system from Saturday will continue to shift \nsoutheastward through the panhandle Sunday. With only around a \nquarter of an inch of rain in 24 hours expected for the northern \nand parts of the central panhandle Sunday, this will mainly be a \nsouthern panhandle issue at this point. The central interior \npanhandle should see around half an inch of rain through the day, \nand the southern interior panhandle may see around an inch or more\nin higher elevations. During this time, Yakutat and parts of the \nnorthern outer coast may see some clearing. Panhandle winds will \nalso be decreasing overnight into Sunday morning, so a majority of\nthe inner channels will be calm by the afternoon. Clarence Strait\nmay see southeasterly fresh breezes linger slightly longer \nthrough the day.\n\nShowers will continue behind the front Sunday, keeping the \npanhandle relatively wet. Another front is expected to move into \nYakutat late Sunday night and continue to shift southeastward over\nthe northern and central panhandle through Monday, reaching the \nsouthern panhandle overnight into Tuesday. In the 48 hours from \nMonday to Tuesday, a majority of the panhandle looks to see 1.5 to\n2 inches of rainfall, with up to 1.5 inches for the southern \ninterior panhandle and higher elevations. Winds will once again \nincrease as this front approaches, though strongest speeds in the \ngulf will remain offshore. Most of the inner channels are looking \nto reach at least a fresh southeasterly breeze, with Lynn Canal \nseeing a higher end strong breeze through midday Monday. Speeds \nwill decrease through Tuesday afternoon and eventually start to \nswitch northerly once the low dips south of the panhandle. \nTemperatures through this period will persist in the upper 50s and\n60s.",
